This is the classic maximum sum bitonic subsequence problem. Check: [1, 15, 51, 100, 18, 9]? Sum = 1+15+51+100+18+9 = 194. Yes, that matches.

Approach: compute max increasing-prefix sums ending at each index (left), and max decreasing-suffix sums starting at each index (right), then answer = max(left[i] + right[i] - arr[i]).
TOOL {"ticket_id": "T-1", "code": "def max_sum(arr):
    n = len(arr)
    if n == 0:
        return 0
    # inc[i]: max sum of increasing subsequence ending at i
    inc = [arr[i] for i in range(n)]
    for i in range(n):
        for j in range(i):
            if arr[j] < arr[i] and inc[j] + arr[i] > inc[i]:
                inc[i] = inc[j] + arr[i]
    # dec[i]: max sum of decreasing subsequence starting at i
    dec = [arr[i] for i in range(n)]
    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):
        for j in range(i + 1, n):
            if arr[j] < arr[i] and dec[j] + arr[i] > dec[i]:
                dec[i] = dec[j] + arr[i]
    return max(inc[i] + dec[i] - arr[i] for i in range(n))
"}

TOOL {"ticket_id": "T-1", "code": "def remove_Occ(s, ch):
    first = s.find(ch)
    if first == -1:
        return s
    last = s.rfind(ch)
    if first == last:
        return s[:first] + s[first+1:]
    return s[:first] + s[first+1:last] + s[last+1:]"}

